ag|none|there is no help switch; an unknown flag is only named back, and its options are -d, -a, -v, -V, -w, -W, -s and -o
perp|none|its one argument is the level to start at; the keys are hjkl, q, S, L and ^C
drawl|none|pipe English text through it; -ing loses its g, vowels broaden into a Texan drawl
lame|none|give it a sentence as arguments; o becomes 0, you becomes U, and so on
canfield|none|name a move by its two ends -- s2, tf, 13, 2f; ht deals, c counts, b bets, q quits
atc|atc -u|
trek|none|it has no help flag; `help' at its Command: prompt lists the commands
arithmetic|arithmetic -h|
monop|none|set up players, then at -- Command: type roll to move, print for the board, quit to end
yahtzee2|none|enter the player count, space toggles human/computer, n names a player; in play a digit holds a die, space rerolls, b shows rules, q quits
dvi2tty|dvi2tty|
disdvi|none|it takes a .DVI file and nothing else -- `-?' is read as a filename, so there is no help flag; the man page is in DOC/dvi2tty
corewar|none|corewar <cycles> <w1.e> <w2.e> runs the fight; assemble warriors with cwasm first; no argument shows usage
cwasm|none|cwasm file.rc [file2.rc ...] writes each warrior as a .e object corewar loads
cwdis|none|cwdis file.e prints the warrior object as an opcode/parameter table
uustat|uustat -?|
browse|browse -?|
almanac|almanac|
zc|none|zc <zipcode> names the city and state; with no argument it opens a form, C clears the field and Q quits
cfscores|none|it takes no options: run it and it reports your canfield bank, or says you have never played
rot22|none|it takes a file name, or reads standard input; there are no options
juggle|juggle -?|
jumble|jumble|
reversi|none|arrow keys or hjkl move the cursor, RETURN places a disk, m opens the game menu; -r resumes a saved game
scrabble|none|hjkl move the cursor, H or V places a word across or down, T trades letters in, A asks the computer for advice, S and R save and restore, Q quits and `?' explains; -n sets the number of players and -m makes one of them the computer
napoleon|none|it takes no options and prints no usage; the prompt is `(napoleon)' and it reads whole sentences -- compass directions move you, `look', `inventory', `get'/`drop', `examine', `read', `score', `save' and `load' do what they say, and `quit' ends the game
gnugo|none|it asks who plays first and the handicap, then takes moves as a letter and number like D4; no options
nethack3|none|it has no help flag; `?' inside the game lists the commands
moria|none|it has no help flag; `?' inside the game lists the commands, and `-s' prints the score list instead of playing
mw|mw -?|
bandit|none|it has no help flag; `y' at its first question prints the instructions, and `q' at the bet prompt quits

wns|wns|
agrep|agrep|
sgrep|sgrep|
dc|none|it has no help flag -- given -? it only says it cannot read its terminal; set DCGRAPHIC=++++|- for plain boxes, and Q or ESC leaves it
sod|sod -?|
rogue|none|it has no help flag; an argument is taken as a saved game to restore
vttest|none|it takes no options and opens its test menu; a test's number and RETURN runs it, 0 leaves
typefast|none|it has no help flag; `y' at its first question explains the game, and `q' at the level prompt quits
castle|none|`castle -h' pages its help file full-screen, RETURN for each page; `?' inside the game shows the command summary
